  "summary": "Phase 1 of review-flexibility is complete and ready to commit. What changed: task config is now an ordinary editable input — live config bytes are no longer validated against a creation-time digest (the six comparisons and the status-side check are gone, along with the error vocabulary), the input fingerprint no longer includes the config digest (with a bounded fallback so in-flight tasks with legacy-recorded fingerprints keep validating, including across config edits), and a last-seen snapshot in task state drives a field-level change notice in status and the semantic view while unparseable config still fails closed. The config template documents editability and overrides. Verification: typecheck clean; 1161 unit, 500 contract, 72 crash/named-integration, and 217 full-integration tests all pass, recorded in the phase transcript; the real-host suite was updated but could not run due to pre-existing breakage (retired local-CLI commands) and is reported as unrun. Reviews: the dispatched counter-review returned no findings after one revision round (a missing file-deletion declaration, fixed); the same-side review found no defects; the constitution gate was approved. Residual risks worth knowing: two server defects were worked around during the workflow (the human_revision crash and the drift-gate/review-continuation deadlock) and are proposed as follow-up fixes; in-flight tasks on other checkouts with legacy fingerprints are a documented limitation (upgrade remains the adoption path). Your choice: authorize the commit of the declared change set to the feature branch, send it back for revision, abort, or cancel.",
  "task_id": "review-flexibility"
}
